    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/honorius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/honorius.jpg" alt="Honorius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/honorius">Honorius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diptych_of_Honorius_(head)_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">unknown Roman artist (diptych) Dea Picture Library (photo), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> were romanized central Europeans who had moved west from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> valley and become foederati of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Under Alaric they sacked Rome in 410, carrying off Galla Placidia, the sister of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honorius">Honorius</a>; his successor Athaulf (410–415) took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> in 413 and married Placidia, after which Honorius enlisted the Visigoths against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, Alans and Suebi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>. In 418 Honorius rewarded his federates under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallia">Wallia</a> (415–418) with land in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a> valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-aquitania">Gallia Aquitania</a>. The settlement probably took place under the system of hospitalitas, and it now seems likely that the Visigoths at first received not great estates but the taxes of the region, which the Gallic aristocrats paid to them instead of to Rome.</p><p>With their capital at Toulouse the Visigoths remained de facto independent and soon expanded at the expense of the feeble Western Empire. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-i">Theodoric I</a> (418–451) attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a> and Narbonne, was beaten by Litorius's Hunnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> in 436, but won the Battle of Toulouse against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> in 439. In 451 Theodoric fought beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Flavius Aetius</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-catalaunian-plains">Battle of the Catalaunian Plains</a> and was killed as the Huns were driven back. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-ii">Theodoric II</a> (453–466), sent into Hispania by the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avitus">Avitus</a>, defeated the Suebic king Rechiarius on the river Órbigo in 456, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Bracara Augusta</a> and took Baetica, Carthaginiensis and southern Lusitania.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sisebut"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sisebut.jpg" alt="Sisebut" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sisebut">Sisebut</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sisebut1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The original uploader was Ex0pos at English Wikipedia., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Visigothic settlement was concentrated in the 5th century along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> held billeting obligations as Roman allies. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euric">Euric</a>'s annexations and above all after Vouillé, Goths settled more freely under their clan rulers, the reiks, who received dukedoms or comital offices, around Mérida, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a>, the bases of Gothic power, and in the farming country between the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a>, the Campi Gothorum. Little Visigothic settlement occurred elsewhere; the Goths were always a minority among the Hispano-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, and by the 7th century the two had become inextricably intertwined, the Gothic language losing its last function as a church language when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a> was renounced in 589.</p><p>Visigothic rule has often been counted part of a dark age, yet the kingdom preserved classical culture. The bathing culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a> continues Romano-Visigothic practice; Reccopolis and Toledo show heavy Byzantine architectural influence, and the votive crowns of the treasure of Guarrazar, buried during the Muslim invasion, survive from treasures that amazed the conquerors. Nobles were taught reading, writing, science, medicine, law and philosophy, as the Institutionum disciplinae confirms; King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisebut">Sisebut</a> wrote poems, one on astronomy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isidore-of-seville">Isidore of Seville</a>'s Etymologies, an encyclopaedia of the age's knowledge, was read throughout medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alaric-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alaric-ii.jpg" alt="Alaric II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alaric-ii">Alaric II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anneau_sigillaire_Alaric_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euric">Euric</a> (466–484), who murdered his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-ii">Theodoric II</a> as Theodoric had murdered Thorismund, carried the kingdom to its greatest reach in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. He fought a series of wars with the Suebi, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerita-augusta">Emerita Augusta</a> in 469, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> Tarraconensis, the last bastion of Western Roman rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, in 472, and by 476 ruled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhone</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>, holding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a> and Marseille; Gallo-Roman and Hispano-Roman aristocrats served him as generals and governors. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a> ended this expansion: in 507, allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a>, they killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaric-ii">Alaric II</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vouille">Battle of Campus Vogladensis</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>.</p><p>In Hispania the kings fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, who carved out the province of Spania during the civil war of 549–554, the Suebi, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a>, Cantabrians and Asturians, and one another. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liuvigild">Liuvigild</a> (569–586) campaigned against the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in the 570s, retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, pacified the north without wholly conquering it and in 585 destroyed the Suebic kingdom in a civil war after King Miro's death; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisebut">Sisebut</a> took Málaga, and Swinthila captured all the Byzantine holdings by 625 while a foothold was gained at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. War against the Basques and Asturians never ceased. In 711 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tariq-ibn-ziyad">Tariq ibn Ziyad</a> landed with about 7,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roderic">Roderic</a> was fighting the Basques; at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalete">Guadalete</a> in late July the king was betrayed by his troops and killed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/recceswinth"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/recceswinth.jpg" alt="Recceswinth" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/recceswinth">Recceswinth</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Corona_de_(29049230050).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ángel M. Felicísimo from Mérida, España, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom was formally recognized as independent, no longer foederati, when Julius Nepos signed an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euric">Euric</a> in 475 granting the lands south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> and west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhone</a> in exchange for military service and Provence. The Balti dynasty ended with Amalaric, murdered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 531 after his defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/childebert-i">Childebert I</a>, and the monarchy became wholly elective; few sons thereafter followed their fathers, and the years 610–631 saw constant regicide. Religious unification under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reccared-i">Reccared I</a> made the church councils at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> the most powerful institution in the state. The fourth council of 633 excommunicated and exiled Sisenand; the councils regulated the election of kings by Gothic noble senators and churchmen, declared the king's person sacred and met regularly on church and political affairs, yet Chintila and Tulga were both deposed.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigothic-code">Liber Iudiciorum</a>, promulgated by Chindaswinth and completed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recceswinth">Recceswinth</a> in 654, replaced all older codes and the separate laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> and Hispano-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> with one law based almost wholly on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a>; women could inherit land and title, dispose of property by will, bear witness in court at fourteen and arrange their own marriages at twenty. The code dropped the harsh laws against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisebut">Sisebut</a> had forced many Jews to convert, and the 17th council of 694 enslaved many more on the charge of conspiracy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/isidore-of-seville"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isidore-of-seville.jpg" alt="Isidore of Seville" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/isidore-of-seville">Isidore of Seville</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Isidor_von_Sevilla.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bartolomé Esteban Murillo, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> carried Roman order into the post-Roman West. Their settlement of 418 made them the first Germanic successor state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, and their kingdom, unlike Vandal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> or Ostrogothic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, held its independence from the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, whose attempts to restore Roman authority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> remained partial and short-lived. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athanagild">Athanagild</a>'s invitation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> during the civil war of 549 brought a Byzantine province of Spania that lasted until 625.</p><p>The kingdom's lasting gifts were legal and ecclesiastical. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigothic-code">Liber Iudiciorum</a>, the Fuero Juzgo of later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, became the basis of Spanish law throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and the Councils of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> set a pattern of church influence on secular affairs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isidore-of-seville">Isidore of Seville</a> became one of the most quoted scholars of the Middle Ages, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodulf-of-orleans">Theodulf of Orléans</a>, fleeing to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-renaissance">Carolingian Renaissance</a>. The Visigoths founded the only new cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> between the 5th and 8th centuries. Their fusion with the Hispano-Romans produced a nobility that thought of itself as one people, the gens Gothorum or Hispani; its remnants fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimania">Septimania</a>, and in Asturias, Joseph F. O'Callaghan writes, joined the native leaders in a new aristocracy. Pelagius's victory at Covadonga in 718 began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a>, and the Kingdom of Asturias traced itself to a Visigothic nobleman elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeps">princeps</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/roderic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roderic.jpg" alt="Roderic" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/roderic">Roderic</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amra5.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown Umayyad artist from 8th century., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The 7th century was filled with civil wars between factions of the aristocracy and struggles between kings and nobles. Liuva II was deposed by Witteric in 603, Swinthila by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, Sisenand by the fourth council, Chintila in 639 and Tulga in 641, and Wamba fell in a bloodless coup in 680. Egica raised his son Wittiza as co-ruler in 698. On Wittiza's death civil war broke out between his sons Achila and Ardo and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roderic">Roderic</a>, who had seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>. The newfound unity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> and Hispano-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> had found expression in ever harsher persecution of outsiders, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, many of whom were enslaved after the council of 694.</p><p>Into this divided kingdom came the Muslim invasion. In 711 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tariq-ibn-ziyad">Tariq ibn Ziyad</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> client of Musa bin Nusair, landed with about 7,000 men while Roderic fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a> in the north. Roderic, betrayed by his troops, died at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalete">Guadalete</a>, and the Muslims took much of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> with little resistance and executed several nobles at Toledo. Musa followed in 712 with 18,000 men, took Mérida in 713 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> and León, still held by King Ardo, in 714, leaving his son Abd al-'Aziz in command. By 716 most of the peninsula was under Islamic rule; Agila II held Tarraconensis and Narbonensis until 714 and Ardo Narbonensis until 721, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimania">Septimania</a> fell between 721 and 725. Only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> resisted, where Pelagius revolted in 718 and won at Covadonga, and the Franks later raised the Marca Hispanica in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Visigothic Kingdom grew from the federates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> in 418 into the ruler of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, lost Gaul to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> at Vouillé in 507 and expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> by 625; Nicene since 589 and governed by the Councils of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigothic-code">Liber Iudiciorum</a>, it fell to the Umayyad invasion of 711, its last king Ardo holding Narbonensis until 721.</em></p>

